---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Btn from '../components/Btn.astro';
import { AUTHOR } from '../config';

/* One entry per correction. Newest first. Never delete an entry: the point of
   a correction log is that the record of the error outlives the error. */
const CORRECTIONS: { date: string; page: string; href: string; was: string; now: string; why: string }[] = [];
---
<Base title="Corrections" description="The correction policy for this site and a dated log of every change made to a published figure or claim.">
<div class="wrap"><Breadcrumbs trail={[{ label: 'Corrections', href: '/corrections/' }]} /></div>
<section class="wrap" style="padding:1.5rem 1.25rem 0">
  <p class="eyebrow">Log</p>
  <h1 style="max-width:20ch">Corrections</h1>
  <p style="max-width:46ch;font-size:1.12rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:1.2rem 0 1.8rem">
    A site that publishes dated figures will get some of them wrong. What separates a reference from a
    blog is what happens next, and whether you can see it happen.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The log</h2>
    {CORRECTIONS.length === 0 ? (
      <p class="note">
        No corrections have been issued yet. This site began publishing dated figures in August 2026;
        the log is empty because it is new, not because nothing will ever appear here. If you find an
        error before we do, the first entry will be yours.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Page</th><th>What changed</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {CORRECTIONS.map(c => (
            <tr>
              <td>{c.date}</td>
              <td><a href={c.href}>{c.page}</a></td>
              <td><strong>Was:</strong> {c.was}<br /><strong>Now:</strong> {c.now}<br /><span class="note">{c.why}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    )}

    <h2>What counts as a correction</h2>
    <p>
      Anything that would change a reader's decision. A wrong fee rate, a wrong effective date, a
      misattributed source, an arithmetic error in a worked example, a claim that turns out not to be
      supported by the source cited for it. All of these get a dated note on the article and an entry here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fixing a typo, a broken link or a clumsy sentence is not a correction and does not get logged.
      Nothing about the substance changed, and a log that fills up with punctuation edits stops being
      useful for the thing it exists to record.
    </p>

    <h2>Updates are not corrections either</h2>
    <p>
      When a marketplace changes a fee, the figure that was published was not wrong — it was true on the
      date it carried. Those pages get a revised modified date and, where the change is material, a note
      explaining what moved and when. That is a normal part of maintaining a dated reference, and it is
      recorded differently from an error on our part. Platform-side changes are tracked on the{' '}
      <a href="/policy-log/">fee and policy log</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What a correction looks like on the page</h2>
    <p>
      The wrong figure is replaced in the body text, so nobody reads the error by accident. A dated note
      is added at the foot of the article stating what the figure previously said, what it says now, and
      why it changed. The article's modified date is updated. Then it is logged here.
    </p>
    <p>
      What does not happen: quietly editing the number and leaving the page looking as though it always
      said that. If a figure here moves, you should be able to find out that it moved.
    </p>

    <h2>How to report one</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`}>{AUTHOR.email}</a>. The report is far more useful with three
      things in it:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>The page, and ideally the section or the sentence.</li>
      <li>What you believe the correct figure or claim is.</li>
      <li>Where it comes from — a link to the marketplace's own documentation is ideal.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Reports are read by <a href="/about/">the author</a>. Where a correction is warranted, the aim is to
      publish it within 48 hours. Where it is not, you will get a reply explaining why, with the source the
      original figure rests on. Disagreement about which of several published figures is current is common
      in this subject and is usually worth the exchange — several pages on this site now carry a table of
      contested figures because two sources could not be reconciled.
    </p>

    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <Btn href="/editorial-standards/">Editorial standards</Btn>
      <Btn href="/policy-log/" quiet={true}>Platform change log</Btn>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
